  "alternatives": [
    {
      "reading": "The distance measured is the loop's, so what the mode byte moves is the feedback path rather than a read-out.",
      "standing": false,
      "ruled_out_by": "The copy is there with the feedback byte at the nought of its printed range. That section returns a distance at the same state and standing as far over the take with nothing in its path as the sections with the loop closed do, so what the output holds a copy of itself at does not need the loop to be there. It is the stage's own latency, and the loop adds further copies at multiples of it -- which is what the second peak of every reading here sits at."
    },
    {
      "reading": "The mode byte moves a length of its own that happens to fall near the ratio the other type's windows fall in.",
      "standing": false,
      "ruled_out_by": "This claim's own criterion, written in round one and met here: every state's ratio holds to the run's own repeats, including the fifth. The alternative predicted at least one state departing by more than those repeats and none does. The repeats are now 0.021 ms rather than the 7.92 to 25.02 the first run had, so the ratio is agreed to about one part in five thousand rather than inside a scatter of a fifth."
    },
    {
      "reading": "The length the mode byte picks is proportional to the other type's window rather than being it.",
      "standing": true,
      "equivalent_under_this_test": true,
      "ruled_out_by": null,
      "separated_by": {
        "observable": "Nothing this archive can measure.",
        "predicts": {
          "the same length": "the five ratios",
          "a length proportional to it": "the five ratios"
        },
        "sensitivity_here": "A test on ratios cannot see a constant multiple, and the constant this run measured is 0.894 rather than one. What would separate them is where the latency sits inside the read-out, which is a place in a chain and not a quantity that leaves the unit.",
        "margin": 1,
        "why_not_separable": "The reading is a ratio and the difference is a constant multiple, so this is recorded as equivalent rather than left open. Refusing to choose between two readings that both reproduce is the answer and not a deferral -- and the multiple itself is published so that a reader who has a use for it is not told it was one.",
        "run": null
      }
    }
  ],
  "refuted_by": "A state of the mode byte whose distance is not the ratio the five lengths stand in, by more than four takes of that state land apart. Every state is now bounded at 0.021 ms, which is the reading's own grid step, so any of the five can refute this and the fifth is no longer the one that cannot.",
  "what_it_would_change": [
    "Rendering this type needs the same table of five lengths the type beside it needs, and not a second one read off its own page. The two print the same `1 - 5` against a byte with no unit beside it and the two answer to the same five ratios.",
    "A byte printed as a bare count of states on this family has now been read as a length twice, on two types, by two quantities and two stages. The measurement that settles such a byte is a sweep of something else while the counted byte is held, and it is cheap."
  ],
  "what_this_does_not_settle": [
    "What the constant multiple is. The five distances are 0.8906 of the five windows to four figures and nothing here says why they are not equal to them: where a latency sits inside a read-out is a place in a chain, and the output cannot be asked it. The figure is sharp enough now to look like a fraction, and it is not written as one here: which fraction it is depends on reading two of the five windows as values that are not whole steps of the clock, and a ratio that resolves into a tidy fraction is a shape this project has retracted before.",
    "Why the reading needs a shift, and why it needs that one. Two semitones up repeats to the reading's grid step; unison scatters by up to 10.52 ms with the strongest peaks in the run; two semitones down returns no peak that clears the roughness at any state. Three behaviours from three settings of one byte, and what the copy is doing at the two that do not read is not established here. The fifth state, which this line used to be about, is settled: it is bounded like the other four and lands on the ratio inside that bound.",
    "The rest of the type. The pitch bytes, the pre-delay, the pan and the balance carry no reading here, and the feedback byte carries one only as the control that showed the copy does not need it."
  ],
