    "why_the_differences": "The multiple is fitted over each state's distance less the first state's, which takes out whatever fixed part the chain adds ahead of the read-out. Fitted on the distances themselves it would be carrying that fixed part as well, and a constant offset read as a multiple is the shape of most of the coincidences this project has had to retract."
  },
  "the_round_that_took_each_state_four_times": {
    "what_was_asked": "Whether the five lengths this byte picks stand in the ratio the other type's windows stand in, or are lengths of their own falling near it. Round one could not say: the figure it quoted as a spread was the spread across three sections held at three different blocks, which is not how far apart one state reads twice, and at the top of the byte it was twenty-two per cent of the distance.",
    "how_the_design_changed_before_the_unit_was_booked": "Before the unit was booked, every take the first run made was read again. The shifted section -- five single takes at two semitones up, taken as a second question because a shifted loop returns a different signal each pass -- stood in the ratio to four figures at all five states. The two unison sections stood in it at neither, and their peaks were the strong ones: forty-eight to sixty-six times the roughness against eight to ten for the shifted ones. A strong peak was pointing at a scattered position, which is the opposite of what the first run's own argument predicted. So the booking was made with the shifted section repeated four times at every state, the unison section repeated three times at every state as the control on it, and one more section at the same shift downwards.",
    "what_came_back": "At two semitones up the reading repeats to its own grid. Four takes of one state land at most 0.021 ms apart, which is one step of the 48 kHz grid the quefrency is read on, and three of the five states returned the same number four times. The five means are 28.505, 38.000, 57.000, 75.995 and 114.026 ms, whose departures from 3 : 4 : 6 : 8 : 12 are 0, -0.007, -0.010, -0.019 and +0.005 ms -- every one inside the floor. At unison the same five states land 0.58, 5.79, 6.60, 5.40 and 10.52 ms apart from themselves.",
    "the_arm_that_returned_nothing": "Two semitones down returns no peak that clears the roughness at any of the five states -- four and a third to five and a half times it, against the six an injected comb was measured to need. That is a reading that could not be made and not a distance that disagreed, and the two are recorded apart on purpose: the strongest peak at two of those states is the same 121.396 ms, which is the signature of a number belonging to the curve rather than to the type. What makes one direction of the same shift readable and the other not is not asked here.",
    "a_guess_that_did_not_survive": "The unison scatter was read off three takes of one state before the booking, and those three sat 4.00 and 3.92 ms apart -- which is 128 steps of the 32000 Hz clock, and looked like a buffer granularity. Twelve more takes do not sit on it: the fifth state's three land 7.83 and 2.69 ms apart. What the unison takes scatter over is not a grid this run can name, and the guess is recorded because it was written down before the run and is the kind that would otherwise be quietly dropped.",
    "what_it_does_not_close": "That these five lengths are the other type's windows rather than five lengths proportional to them, which was already recorded as equivalent under this test and still is: the reading is a ratio and the difference is a constant multiple. What the run does change about it is the multiple's own sharpness, from a range of 0.891 to 0.908 to a constant 0.8906.",
